WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
The first film followed atoms through connected cycles.

2
00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.020
What happens when people make one flow much larger than before?

3
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.440
Return to the same watershed and watch the amounts change.

4
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:20.090
A flow is matter moving between reservoirs during some time.

5
00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.630
Picture each reservoir with an amount meter.

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.850
Arrows show matter entering or leaving.

7
00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:31.790
Their widths show relative flow, and every amount here is schematic.

8
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:37.533
If matter enters and leaves at matching rates, the amount can stay steady.

9
00:00:38.813 --> 00:00:40.313
That does not mean atoms stop.

10
00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:45.193
Tokens keep crossing both boundaries while the meter remains level.

11
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:51.767
Coal, oil, and natural gas hold carbon that was underground for millions of years.

12
00:00:53.127 --> 00:00:56.627
Combustion moves that old carbon into today's air quickly.

13
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:02.927
Plants and the ocean take up some added carbon, but combustion adds it faster than

14
00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:03.927
they remove it.

15
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.180
Start from the balanced tank.

16
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.780
Now the incoming flow grows wider, while the outgoing flow stays the same.

17
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.120
What happens to the amount in the reservoir?

18
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.540
Hold your answer.

19
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:27.073
The amount rises, because now more matter enters than leaves.

20
00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:29.833
The atoms were not created.

21
00:01:30.993 --> 00:01:35.313
They moved from another reservoir, and the receiving reservoir gained them.

22
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:40.337
People also add usable nitrogen to fields as fertilizer.

23
00:01:41.697 --> 00:01:46.137
Rain and flowing water can carry some nitrogen toward streams and the Bay.

24
00:01:47.497 --> 00:01:50.557
This human-added flow rides inside the water cycle.

25
00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:54.737
Not every added nitrogen atom takes the same route.

26
00:01:55.977 --> 00:02:01.157
Some stays on land, some enters living things, some reaches water, and some returns

27
00:02:01.157 --> 00:02:02.277
to the air as gas.

28
00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:08.337
The important question is how the added flow changes each reservoir over time.

29
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:12.117
How can scientists tell whether a cycle changed?

30
00:02:13.277 --> 00:02:18.277
A measurement records something observed, such as carbon dioxide in sampled air.

31
00:02:19.537 --> 00:02:25.237
A model combines observations and assumptions to estimate flows that cannot all be

32
00:02:25.237 --> 00:02:26.237
measured directly.

33
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.087
Each kind of evidence has limits.

34
00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:32.427
One station is not the whole atmosphere.

35
00:02:33.687 --> 00:02:37.307
A watershed model estimates rather than measures every stream.

36
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:42.047
Its result also depends on which sources the model includes.

37
00:02:43.217 --> 00:02:45.597
Use each source for the question it can answer.

38
00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:50.077
A measured record shows what changed at its sampling place.

39
00:02:51.357 --> 00:02:56.517
A model can compare estimated sources across a watershed, if its boundaries and

40
00:02:56.517 --> 00:02:57.757
assumptions stay clear.

41
00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:03.777
Carbon and nitrogen follow different processes, yet the same reasoning applies.

42
00:03:05.257 --> 00:03:10.317
Identify the reservoir, compare what enters with what leaves, and ask which process

43
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:11.317
changed the flow.

44
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:16.330
When more matter enters a reservoir than leaves it, the amount rises.

45
00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:22.950
The next step is evidence: compare measured records with modeled flows, then keep

46
00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.250
their different limits in view.
